// Lets Pylance/Pyright resolve `agent_review` in cli/tests/*.py without
// depending on whether `pip install -e cli[dev]` has been run in the
// active interpreter. Every test file does
// `sys.path.insert(0, .../cli/src)` before importing, which works at
// runtime (pytest executes it before the import) but static analyzers
// don't execute code, so they can't see it -- extraPaths tells Pyright
// the same thing statically.
//
// cli/tests is here too, for the same reason: scripts/validate_fixtures.py
// (a repo-root script, not part of the cli/ package) does
// `sys.path.insert(0, .../cli/tests)` before `from support.cassette import
// ...` -- support/ is test-only code, never installed -- and needs the
// same static-vs-runtime bridge mypy.ini's own `mypy_path = cli/tests`
// already provides for mypy.
//
// Pyright applies a built-in default exclude list (**/node_modules,
// **/__pycache__, **/.*) only when "exclude" is left unset -- defining a
// custom "exclude" here, as this file already did for tests/fixtures/,
// replaces those defaults rather than adding to them, so they're listed
// out explicitly below too (Pylance's own "missing default excludes"
// suggestion). Without this, __pycache__/.mypy_cache/.pytest_cache/
// .ruff_cache/.venv-style dot-directories that accumulate locally would
// be back in Pyright's whole-workspace scan.
//
// tests/fixtures/ is listed under BOTH "exclude" and "ignore", not just
// "exclude" alone. Every case in there is a synthetic diff snippet --
// `scripts/validate_fixtures.py`'s build_case_diff() only ever diffs these
// files as text (git diff --no-index), never imports or executes them --
// so on purpose they reference modules/names that don't exist anywhere in
// the repo (e.g. performance-review/true_positive/orders.py's bare `db`/
// `Order`, or api-type-contract-review's *_architecture cases importing
// `billing.ledger`/`db.orders_repository`, which exist only as sibling
// fixture text, not as an importable package). "exclude" alone stops
// Pyright/Pylance from walking these files during a whole-workspace scan,
// but does NOT suppress diagnostics for one of them once it's individually
// open in the editor -- confirmed 2026-09-22 when a newly added fixture
// still surfaced "import could not be resolved" in VS Code despite the
// pre-existing "exclude" entry. "ignore" is the stronger directive that
// also silences diagnostics on an explicitly opened file, which is what
// this directory actually needs.
{
  "extraPaths": ["cli/src", "cli/tests"],
  "exclude": ["**/node_modules", "**/__pycache__", "**/.*", "tests/fixtures", ".venv"],
  "ignore": ["tests/fixtures"]
}
